    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d102">

                <pb facs="0160" n="148" xml:id="pg148"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/8–2045</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d102-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-08-20T12:00:00+03:00">August 20,
                            1945—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received August 20—4:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2960. Dept’s 1777, Aug 9 and 1868, Aug 19. Substance of communiqué on supervision
                    Greek elections and reassuring observations contained in Dept’s 1861 Aug 18 have
                    been communicated to Molotov in letter this morning.</p>
                <p>To Dept 2960, rptd Athens 42, London 415, Paris 315.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d102-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d102-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 3020, August 23, 1945, 2 p.m., Ambassador Harriman reported that
                    “Vyshinski under date of August 21 acknowledged my letter of August 20 but
                    offered no comments.” (868.00/8–2345) Audrey Yanuaryevich Vishinsky was First
                    Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
